    "document_id": "c0c15abd-c9fc-4e92-a77b-3193b2116cbb",
    "origin_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/cargo-thieves-are-chasing-ai-hardware-down-the-dirt-road-to-the-data-center",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-23T16:37:54.172815+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/cargo-thieves-are-chasing-ai-hardware-down-the-dirt-road-to-the-data-center",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/cargo-thieves-are-chasing-ai-hardware-down-the-dirt-road-to-the-data-center",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-11-23T16:25:44+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-23T16:25:44+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-23T16:39:24.372478+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-23T16:39:24.372478+00:00",
    "body": "The AI infrastructure boom is drawing capital at a pace the freight industry has never had to secure. It is also drawing organized crime.\nDavid Warrick, EVP of Enterprise at Overhaul, has spent more than 30 years in supply chain, including two decades at Microsoft running supply chains for the original Xbox and the Nokia acquisition. He retired in 2022 and then joined Overhaul, the global leader in supply chain risk detection, response, and resolution. In a recent conversation, he described a threat landscape shaped by scarcity, scale and old-fashioned deception.\nA build-out without precedent\nWarrick put the scale in historical terms. The U.S. interstate highway system took 37 years and roughly $650 billion to build, he said. AI infrastructure spending now runs about double that figure every year across the globe, with a high percentage landing in the U.S.\nWarrick said the vulnerability in that spending is not the facility itself. “The bright and shiny data center sitting on the hill is not the problem. It’s the dirt road leading to the data center, which is where all of the challenges really are.”\nThat road now carries far more value than it used to. Warrick said that five years ago, the precious cargo in cargo-theft conversations was smartphones, with a pallet worth about a million dollars. “Now we’re seeing $17 million pallets, $20 million pallets filled with Blackwell chips,” he said, adding that “you’ve now got $100 million truckloads moving across the country.”\nScarcity spreads the risk\nThe exposure extends well beyond GPUs and memory. Demand has made nearly every component of a data center harder to find, Warrick said. “It’s across all aspects of the build out of the infrastructure: generators, transformers, bus bars, high voltage switchgear, low voltage switchgear.”\nWhen supply is tight, he said, even parts that look like commodities become worth stealing. A secondary market follows, and criminals have noticed. “There’s a big secondary market for them because of this year’s scarcity,” he said.\nHandoffs and hijacked carriers\nAccording to Warrick, the weak points are the same ones that have long plagued high-value freight. “Anywhere where there’s a handoff in the supply chain tends to open up a level of weakness.”\nWhat has changed is the sophistication of the people exploiting them. Warrick described groups that buy their way into legitimacy. “These are criminal gangs that have bought legitimate trucking companies and have been running them for some time as a legitimate business, and then started to gain traction on the load boards because people are not paying attention.”\nThe most common play is the simplest. Warrick described it as “fake paperwork from a driver, a legitimate trucking company who’s been subcontracted twice and then picks up a load, and then just disappears.” He said the fraudulent pickup is the same method already plaguing consumer electronics, beverage and healthcare freight.\nMore elaborate tactics are in circulation too. “We’ve seen criminals who have quickly resprayed a truck. They’ve changed the DOT numbers. They’ve switched the license plates.” Warrick said thieves have also turned to electronic countermeasures. “We’ve seen them use signal jammers,” he said, noting that much track-and-trace capability depends on truck telemetry and IoT devices placed along roads. In his view, there is “no typical MO in this one.”\nWhy the economics favor the thief\nWarrick framed the threat as industrial rather than opportunistic. “These are transnational organized criminal gangs. Think cartels, right?” He said such groups operate with finance and HR departments and weigh their options accordingly. “A pallet of smartphones for a million dollars, or a pallet with some Blackwell chips on it for $15 million, which are you going to take?”\nThe goods themselves add to the appeal. Unlike narcotics, chips are legal to hold and move. “It’s easy to ship these things around even across borders because they’re not illegal goods,” Warrick said. He pointed to Russia, Iran and China as destinations of concern, particularly given denied-party screening issues.\nShipper behavior can compound the problem. Warrick said some shippers, wary of building a $100 million truckload, are shifting to smaller vehicles such as transit vans and box trucks. The logic is sound from a transportation standpoint, he said, but criminals see something else. “I can pick up a Sprinter van with a $15 million pallet in the back,” he said, describing how a thief might think about it.\nA loss that goes beyond the cargo\nFor most shippers, the cost of a theft is more than the product itself. Warrick said that outside AI infrastructure, customers tell Overhaul that “the monetary value typically doesn’t even hit in the top four reasons” they worry about. Customer satisfaction, empty shelves and brand damage usually rank higher.\nAI infrastructure is different. Warrick said the stolen item is often part of a just-in-time build with no slack in it. “If I lose a specific component, I don’t have an automatic way of being able to replace that because everything’s already been bought and sold for the next number of years.”\nThat means a theft can delay compute capacity that has already been contracted. “Any disruption to that has a direct monetary impact to the hyperscaler or the neo cloud provider or whoever it is,” Warrick said. He also said many large thefts never become public, partly because “there’s a level of embarrassment around this.” He noted that thefts “into the 10s of millions of dollars” have occurred in recent weeks.\nAnatomy of a recovery\nWarrick described one recent recovery, without naming the parties. A well-known carrier had subcontracted a load, and the pickup turned out to be fraudulent. Overhaul was tracking the shipment. “We were watching the load. We noticed an unscheduled stop.” The team contacted the driver and the shipper’s side, and the explanations seemed legitimate at first but something felt off.\nOverhaul’s analytics flagged the load as well. Warrick said the company has built up a data set from tracking cargo across the industry and is “adding about 200 million data points every month.” When those signals lined up with the human suspicion, the team worked with local law enforcement to intercept the load and recover it. The driver was taken away.\nWarrick said the model is about more than alerts. “It’s more than just dots on a map,” he said, adding that “we manage the entire process.” He said Overhaul works closely with local agencies on the ground around the world. “This problem is not getting smaller,” he said.\nDeterrence is still a gap\nRecovery is only part of the answer, Warrick said. He cautioned that “we’ve got some work to do, even from a legislation perspective,” and said the goal should be “a punishment that sticks” so that future offenders think twice. He said he expects the trend to continue, since more money is flowing into the build-out.\nTreat cargo like cash\nWarrick’s advice for shippers was to slow down and verify. “If you think about your shipments not as inventory but as cash, you would pay a lot more attention if you had a pallet loaded with dollar bills.”\nHe said that mindset should translate into process. “It’s okay to take your time, and it’s okay to check and double check.” He encouraged shippers not to hesitate to consult law enforcement or intelligence specialists when something looks wrong, and to treat chain of custody as a front-line control rather than a paperwork exercise.\nHis final point was about instinct. “If at any point in time something feels off, then just take a breath and actually double check again, because that’s going to be way better than trying to explain $100 million loss.”\nTo learn more about Overhaul, visit https://www.over-haul.com/",
